Build an interactive web experience called:

THE UNFINISHED MAP OF BUTTERFLY ISLAND

This is a hidden interactive page for a transmedia storyworld.

CORE CONCEPT
The page begins as an unfinished antique map of Butterfly Island.
Visitors gradually construct their own interpretation of the island by adding locations, routes, notes and clues based on fragments they have discovered elsewhere.

There must NEVER be an official completed geography.

Different users may interpret the same location differently.

--------------------------------------------------
1. TECHNICAL SETUP
--------------------------------------------------

Build this as a static website suitable for GitHub Pages.

Use:
- HTML
- CSS
- JavaScript
- SVG and/or HTML Canvas for drawing interaction

Do not require a backend for the first prototype.

Save the user's map locally using localStorage so their work remains after refreshing the page.

Make the experience work primarily on desktop/laptop, but keep the layout responsive.

Organize the code clearly into:
index.html
style.css
script.js

--------------------------------------------------
2. VISUAL DIRECTION
--------------------------------------------------

The interface should look like an old explorer's map, not a modern digital map.

Visual references:
- antique pirate maps
- old expedition maps
- hand-drawn cartography
- aged parchment
- faded ink
- imperfect handwritten annotations
- hand-drawn mountains, forests, rivers and settlements
- dotted expedition routes
- compass rose
- old archival documents

Avoid:
- Google Maps aesthetics
- modern map pins
- glossy UI
- bright modern interface colors
- fantasy game HUD aesthetics
- polished vector-map appearance

The map should feel physical, imperfect and discovered.

Use a warm parchment background with subtle paper texture, stains, faded edges and very light grain.

Interface controls should resemble small archival/cartography tools rather than modern app buttons.

--------------------------------------------------
3. THE BASE MAP
--------------------------------------------------

Place one large island in the center of the map.

CRITICAL:
The island must clearly have the silhouette of a BUTTERFLY.

It should have:
- two large upper wings
- two smaller lower wings
- a narrow central body-like land connection

However, do not make it look like a literal cartoon butterfly.
It should still look like a believable naturally formed island whose coastline happens to resemble a butterfly.

The island should occupy approximately 60–70% of the main map canvas.

Initially the island should contain almost no confirmed geography.

The starting map may include only:
- coastline
- subtle terrain lines
- faint mountains
- faint rivers
- sparse forest markings
- ocean wave marks
- a compass rose

DO NOT initially label:
- Eyeon
- villages
- forests
- Iris Fenwick's outpost
- the Daughter's route
- political regions

Those must be interpreted and added by the visitor.

--------------------------------------------------
4. PAGE INTRO
--------------------------------------------------

Before entering the map, show:

THE UNFINISHED MAP

"No complete map of Butterfly Island exists."

"Build the island from what you have seen, heard, and believed."

Button:

ENTER THE MAP

Use a subtle paper-opening or map-unfolding transition when entering.

--------------------------------------------------
5. MAIN INTERFACE
--------------------------------------------------

The map should occupy most of the screen.

Create a compact vertical cartographer toolbar on the LEFT side.

Tools:

HAND
PEN
ROUTE
LOCATION
NOTE
CLUE
ERASER

Also include:

UNDO
REDO
ZOOM IN
ZOOM OUT
RESET VIEW

At the top/right provide:

MY MAP
COMMUNITY MAP

For the first prototype, COMMUNITY MAP can display example community interpretations or remain marked as experimental.

--------------------------------------------------
6. HAND / NAVIGATION TOOL
--------------------------------------------------

The Hand tool allows the visitor to move around the map.

Features:
- click and drag to pan
- mouse wheel / trackpad zoom
- smooth zoom centered near cursor
- minimum and maximum zoom limits
- cursor changes to open hand
- while dragging, cursor becomes closed hand

Drawing must be disabled while Hand mode is active.

--------------------------------------------------
7. PEN TOOL — VERY IMPORTANT
--------------------------------------------------

The Pen must feel like drawing with an old cartographer's ink pen.

Do NOT implement it as a basic ugly browser drawing line.

Create smooth freehand drawing.

Requirements:

- pointer/mouse/touch drawing
- smooth curves rather than jagged straight segments
- interpolate pointer movement
- simplify excessive points after stroke completion
- rounded stroke caps and joins
- slight natural variation in stroke width
- subtle opacity variation
- dark brown/sepia ink rather than pure black

Default pen:
approximately 2px at normal map scale.

Provide 3 pen thicknesses:

FINE
REGULAR
BOLD

Fine:
for small annotations/details

Regular:
default map drawing

Bold:
for major boundaries or emphasized routes

Add a small circular preview showing current pen thickness.

The stroke must scale correctly with map zoom.

When the map is zoomed, drawing coordinates must remain attached to the correct geographical point.

The line must NOT shift position after zooming or panning.

Optional:
If pointer pressure information is available, use pressure to subtly affect stroke width.
If pressure is unavailable, simulate very subtle width variation based on drawing speed.

Keep the effect restrained.
It should resemble an ink line, not a digital brush.

--------------------------------------------------
8. ROUTE TOOL
--------------------------------------------------

The Route tool is separate from the Pen.

It creates expedition/travel paths.

Visual style:
hand-drawn dotted or dashed route line.

Interaction:

1. User clicks a starting point.
2. User clicks additional points.
3. A curved route connects them.
4. Double-click or press ENTER to finish.

The route should not create perfectly straight geometric lines.

Use slightly curved paths between points.

Provide route styles:

DOTTED
DASHED
ROUGH INK

Allow the visitor to click an existing route and edit/delete it.

Routes may cross and contradict other routes.

This is intentional.

--------------------------------------------------
9. LOCATION TOOL
--------------------------------------------------

When LOCATION is selected:

1. Cursor becomes a small crosshair.
2. Visitor clicks anywhere on Butterfly Island.
3. Open a small parchment-style popup.

Fields:

LOCATION NAME
TYPE
INTERPRETATION
SOURCE / CLUE
CONFIDENCE

TYPE options:
City
Village
Forest
Mountain
River
Ruins
Outpost
Unknown

CONFIDENCE options:
Theory
Likely
Certain

Button:
PLACE ON MAP

After placement, create a small hand-drawn cartographic marker.

Do NOT use modern Google-style map pins.

Use small illustrated symbols such as:
- settlement
- tower
- tree
- mountain
- tent/outpost
- question mark for unknown

Display the location name underneath in a handwritten/cartographic serif style.

--------------------------------------------------
10. NOTES
--------------------------------------------------

NOTE allows the visitor to place interpretation notes anywhere on the map.

Click map → small text editor appears.

Example:

"I think the forest mentioned in Reel 06 begins here."

Notes should look like handwritten marginal annotations directly on an old map.

Allow:
- edit
- move
- rotate slightly
- delete

Do not automatically verify whether the interpretation is correct.

--------------------------------------------------
11. CLUES / SOURCES
--------------------------------------------------

CLUE places a small paper fragment or archival marker.

Clicking it opens:

SOURCE
DETAIL
RELATED LOCATION

Example sources:

Instagram — Reel 06
Instagram — Reel 09
Audio Drama — EP03
Campaign Material
Election Website
Unknown Source

A clue represents evidence the visitor used to construct their map.

Clues should visually resemble:
- tiny paper scraps
- pinned notes
- faded archival markers

NOT modern UI icons.

--------------------------------------------------
12. ERASER
--------------------------------------------------

The eraser should delete user-created content only.

It must NOT erase the original Butterfly Island base map.

Allow erasing:
- pen strokes
- routes
- locations
- notes
- clues

When hovering over an erasable object, subtly highlight it.

Click to remove.

Undo must restore erased objects.

--------------------------------------------------
13. UNDO / REDO
--------------------------------------------------

Implement a proper action history.

Undo and Redo should work for:

drawing
erasing
adding locations
moving locations
adding notes
editing notes
drawing routes
deleting routes
adding clues

Keyboard shortcuts:

CTRL/CMD + Z = Undo
CTRL/CMD + SHIFT + Z = Redo

--------------------------------------------------
14. MY MAP
--------------------------------------------------

MY MAP contains only the current visitor's interpretation.

Everything they create should persist in localStorage.

Include:

SAVE MAP
EXPORT MAP
CLEAR MY MAP

EXPORT MAP should allow the visitor to export their interpretation as a PNG image.

Before CLEAR MY MAP, require confirmation.

--------------------------------------------------
15. COMMUNITY MAP
--------------------------------------------------

The conceptual purpose of COMMUNITY MAP is to show that different people imagine Butterfly Island differently.

Do NOT merge interpretations into one authoritative geography.

Instead, community contributions should visually overlap.

For the prototype, create several example interpretations.

Example:

User A places "Forest" northwest of Eyeon.
User B places "Forest" northeast.
User C draws a completely different route.

All three may coexist.

Use slightly faded layers when multiple interpretations overlap.

Clicking a contribution should reveal:

"Community Interpretation"

rather than:

"Confirmed Location"

--------------------------------------------------
16. IMPORTANT NARRATIVE RULE
--------------------------------------------------

NEVER tell the visitor:

"Correct"
"Incorrect"
"Official location"
"True route"

The map is about interpretation.

Contradictions are allowed.

The experience should make the visitor feel that Butterfly Island exists differently depending on how its stories are interpreted.

--------------------------------------------------
17. SMALL DETAILS
--------------------------------------------------

Add subtle atmospheric details:

- map title printed into parchment
- compass rose
- faint ocean lines
- tiny decorative sea illustrations
- coordinate-like border marks that do NOT correspond to real coordinates
- slightly torn paper edges
- faint ink stains

When zooming deeply into the island, paper texture and drawn terrain should remain visually coherent.

Keep animations subtle.

No excessive fantasy effects.

--------------------------------------------------
18. FINAL EXPERIENCE
--------------------------------------------------

The visitor should experience this sequence:

ENTER SECRET PAGE
↓
SEE THE UNFINISHED BUTTERFLY-SHAPED ISLAND
↓
EXPLORE
↓
PLACE LOCATIONS FROM STORY CLUES
↓
DRAW THE DAUGHTER'S POSSIBLE JOURNEY
↓
ADD NOTES AND SOURCES
↓
COMPARE THEIR INTERPRETATION WITH OTHERS
↓
REALIZE THAT THERE IS NO SINGLE CONFIRMED MAP

The finished visual result should gradually resemble an old, heavily annotated explorer's map filled with hand-drawn locations, dotted routes, notes and conflicting interpretations.

Do not create a completed map for the visitor.

Give them the tools to construct one.
